  "course_key": "g3-sec1-math",
  "unit_key": "g3-sec1-n6",
  "position": 1,
  "status": "draft",
  "title": "Cartesian coordinates in two dimensions",
  "summary": "Read, plot and transform points in four quadrants, then use coordinate differences to solve geometric problems.",
  "outcomes": [
    "6.1"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [],
  "estimated_minutes": 50,
  "objectives": [
    "Read and interpret ordered pairs in all four quadrants.",
    "Use coordinates to describe translations, reflections and midpoints.",
    "Find horizontal and vertical lengths, perimeters and areas from coordinates."
  ],
  "mastery_policy_key": "n6-default-v1",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-coordinates-method",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Read an ordered pair from the axes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "An ordered pair is written (x, y). Start at the origin, move horizontally to the x-coordinate, and then move vertically to the y-coordinate. Reversing the order usually names a different point. A point on the x-axis has y = 0, a point on the y-axis has x = 0, and the origin is (0, 0)."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "\\mathrm{I}:(+,+),\\quad \\mathrm{II}:(-,+),\\quad \\mathrm{III}:(-,-),\\quad \\mathrm{IV}:(+,-)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The quadrant sign pattern is a check, not a replacement for reading the scales. First identify the value represented by one grid interval, especially when an axis is marked in twos, halves or another scale."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "n6-lesson-01-coordinate-plane"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-coordinate-transformations",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Describe movement and reflection with coordinates",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A translation adds the same horizontal and vertical changes to every point. Moving h units right and k units up maps (x, y) to (x + h, y + k); a negative value means left or down. The shape, size and orientation stay unchanged."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "(x,y)\\xrightarrow{\\text{translation by }(h,k)}(x+h,y+k)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A reflection changes the coordinate perpendicular to the mirror line. In the x-axis, y changes sign: (x, y) becomes (x, -y). In the y-axis, x changes sign: (x, y) becomes (-x, y). A point on the mirror line remains fixed. In the diagram, A and A-prime have opposite x-coordinates and the same y-coordinate."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-coordinate-measurement",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Measure lengths and locate midpoints",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For a horizontal segment, subtract the x-coordinates and use the positive distance. For a vertical segment, subtract the y-coordinates. These distances can be combined to find the perimeter and area of an axis-aligned rectangle."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "\\text{horizontal length}=|x_2-x_1|,\\qquad \\text{vertical length}=|y_2-y_1|"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The midpoint is halfway in both directions, so average the x-coordinates and average the y-coordinates. Keep brackets around negative values before adding. A quick check is that the midpoint must lie between the two endpoint coordinates on each axis."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "M=\\left(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2}\\right)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-coordinates-example",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: rectangle from four coordinates",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A rectangle has vertices (−4, 3), (3, 3), (3, −2) and (−4, −2). Find its perimeter and area."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Find the horizontal side using the two x-values."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "|3-(-4)|=7"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Find the vertical side using the two y-values."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "|3-(-2)|=5"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Use the perimeter and area formulas."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "P=2(7+5)=24\\text{ units},\\qquad A=7\\times5=35\\text{ square units}"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Opposite sides have equal coordinate differences; 7 + 5 + 7 + 5 = 24, and a 7-by-5 rectangle has area 35."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-coordinate-transform-example",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: translate, reflect and find a midpoint",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Point P(-5, 2) is translated 7 units right and 3 units down to Q. Q is then reflected in the x-axis to R. Find Q, R and the midpoint of QR."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Add 7 to x and subtract 3 from y."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "Q=(-5+7,\\ 2-3)=(2,-1)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Reflection in the x-axis keeps x and reverses the sign of y."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "R=(2,1)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Average corresponding coordinates for the midpoint."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "M=\\left(\\frac{2+2}{2},\\frac{-1+1}{2}\\right)=(2,0)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Q and R are one unit below and above the x-axis, so their midpoint (2, 0) lies on the mirror line."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-coordinates-recall",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Point A(-4, 7) is reflected in the y-axis. State the x-coordinate of its image."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "4",
        "canonical_latex": "4",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Reflection in the y-axis changes -4 to 4 and keeps the y-coordinate unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "n6-lesson-01-summary",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Method checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Read x before y and check the quadrant signs against the axis scales. For translations, add the stated movement; for reflections, change only the coordinate perpendicular to the mirror axis."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use absolute coordinate differences for horizontal and vertical lengths. Average matching coordinates for a midpoint, then check that the result lies halfway between the endpoints."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
